1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,480
A user asks a coding assistant to fix a

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:06,040
failing test.

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:07,880
Along with the question, the server

4
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:10,160
receives a system prompt listing the

5
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:12,320
tools the model may call. The server

6
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:14,000
also receives the file that failed, the

7
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:15,720
error the test printed, and the

8
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,960
conversation so far.

9
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:20,400
Joined into one block, the prompt runs

10
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:22,840
to a few thousand tokens.

11
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:24,560
The prompt arrives as a single request.

12
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:26,800
From arrival to the last token of the

13
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:29,080
answer, the request occupies a slot

14
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:31,480
somewhere in the rack.

15
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:34,280
The request is headed for a rack of 72

16
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:36,640
Blackwell GPUs. An NVLink fabric carries

17
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:38,160
130 terabytes per second between those

18
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:41,120
GPUs.

19
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:43,520
Because the fabric moves data that fast,

20
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:46,240
NVIDIA describes the whole rack as a

21
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:50,160
single massive GPU.

22
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:52,120
The rack holds 13.4 terabytes of memory.

23
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,600
The model being served holds a trillion

24
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:56,240
parameters.

25
00:00:53,600 --> 00:01:00,080
Stored at 4 bits each, a trillion

26
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:01,680
parameters come to 500 GB, under 4% of

27
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:04,440
the rack's memory.

28
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:06,520
That leaves 12.9 terabytes for key value

29
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:10,080
cache, the attention state every

30
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:12,440
conversation reuses for each new token.

31
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:14,680
A gateway picks a machine that already

32
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:17,120
holds part of the prompt.

33
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:19,800
Prefill reads the remaining tokens in

34
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:21,760
one pass and builds the key value cache

35
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:23,960
for this request.

36
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:26,640
The key value cache crosses the network

37
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:29,160
to a separate pool of machines.

38
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:32,320
Those machines run decode, writing the

39
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:35,280
rest of the answer one token at a time.

40
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:38,560
At each step, decode chooses only from

41
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:41,960
tokens that keep the tool call valid.

42
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:44,440
A Blackwell GPU in this rack carries out

43
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:48,000
10 quadrillion 4-bit operations every

44
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:50,680
second. NVIDIA advertises twice that

45
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,960
rate, counting a sparsity mode that

46
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:55,480
skips zeroed weights, which this

47
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:58,320
workload does not use.

48
00:01:55,480 --> 00:02:00,840
The same GPU pulls about 8 terabytes out

49
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:02,920
of its own memory every second.

50
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:05,320
Divide 10 quadrillion operations by 8

51
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:08,320
terabytes, and the arithmetic units need

52
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:10,440
1,250 operations from every byte fetched

53
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:13,160
to stay busy.

54
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:15,040
One byte holds two 4-bit weights.

55
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:17,080
Multiplying and accumulating each of

56
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:18,560
those two weights once is four

57
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:21,280
operations.

58
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:23,760
Getting to 1,250 means the same byte has

59
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:25,840
to serve roughly 300 tokens before the

60
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:28,080
chip lets it go.

61
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:31,360
That gap is the memory wall, which we

62
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:33,480
covered in detail in episode 1.

63
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:35,440
The request reaches the gateway,

64
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:38,080
an ordinary load balancer of the kind

65
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:40,160
that fronts any web service.

66
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:42,680
Load balancers normally pick a machine

67
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:45,440
by rotation or by whichever machine is

68
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:48,760
least busy. This gateway picks by what

69
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:51,000
each machine already holds in memory.

70
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:53,240
Most of this prompt is a prefix some

71
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,880
machine already holds.

72
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:56,880
The system prompt and the tool

73
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:58,400
definitions do not change between

74
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,480
sessions.

75
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,360
The earlier turns of this conversation

76
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:04,840
were pre-filled a minute ago when the

77
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:06,640
user asked the previous question.

78
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:08,680
Only the newest question has never been

79
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:10,120
through the model.

80
00:03:08,680 --> 00:03:12,519
The gateway sends the request to the

81
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:14,519
machine holding that prefix. That

82
00:03:12,519 --> 00:03:17,000
machine still has the prefix in its key

83
00:03:14,519 --> 00:03:19,239
value cache because the engine keeps a

84
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,160
finished requests cache instead of

85
00:03:19,239 --> 00:03:23,280
discarding it.

86
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:25,200
So, prefill starts partway through the

87
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:27,800
prompt at the first token that the

88
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:29,640
machine has not seen.

89
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,680
The machine that takes the request does

90
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:35,360
nothing but prefill.

91
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:37,640
Decode runs on a different set of GPUs.

92
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:39,600
Inside, the uncached tokens go through

93
00:03:37,640 --> 00:03:42,440
the model once. The weights arrive

94
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:44,320
4-bits wide. Each block of 16 weights

95
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:46,560
carries its own scale.

96
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:48,959
The matrix multiply instruction applies

97
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:51,360
that scale as it runs, so the weights

98
00:03:48,959 --> 00:03:53,280
stay 4-bits wide until the instant they

99
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:55,840
are multiplied.

100
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:57,720
Flash attention 4, the attention kernel

101
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:58,960
written for Blackwell, computes the

102
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:01,200
attention.

103
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:02,352
Each matrix multiply is split across

104
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:02,560
several GPUs,

105
00:04:02,352 --> 00:04:04,520
>> [music]

106
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:06,360
>> which exchange partial results over

107
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:08,520
NVLink.

108
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:11,120
Each layer of the model holds hundreds

109
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:13,080
of separate expert networks. A router

110
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:14,560
sends every token to eight of those

111
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:16,519
experts.

112
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:19,280
The eight experts a token needs sit on

113
00:04:16,519 --> 00:04:21,760
different GPUs, so the tokens cross in

114
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:24,320
an all-to-all exchange, every GPU

115
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:26,480
sending to every other.

116
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:29,080
Prefill [music] ends with a key value

117
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:31,120
cache covering the whole prompt.

118
00:04:29,080 --> 00:04:32,880
Prefill also produces the first token of

119
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:34,680
the answer.

120
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:37,080
The rest of the answer is generated on

121
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:39,480
the decode machines, so a copy of that

122
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:42,240
cache has to cross to them.

123
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:45,640
The prefix stays behind here, ready for

124
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:47,080
the next request that shares it.

125
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:50,000
The cache has to reach the decode

126
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:51,800
machines before generation can continue.

127
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,600
Multi-head latent attention decides how

128
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,919
large that cache is.

129
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:58,520
Standard attention stores a full key and

130
00:04:55,919 --> 00:05:00,720
value for every attention head of every

131
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:03,160
token in every layer.

132
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:07,200
Latent attention stores one short vector

133
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:09,480
per layer instead, about 1,100 bytes.

134
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:12,240
Across 61 layers, that comes to 70

135
00:05:09,480 --> 00:05:13,960
kilobytes for each token in the prompt.

136
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:15,880
A prompt of a few thousand tokens

137
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:17,960
therefore makes a cache of a few hundred

138
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:20,000
megabytes.

139
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:22,280
Moving a few hundred megabytes between

140
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,320
machines is normally work for both

141
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:27,520
operating systems.

142
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:29,680
Remote direct memory access skips them.

143
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:31,960
The network card on the prefill machine

144
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:33,640
reads the cache straight out of memory

145
00:05:31,960 --> 00:05:35,720
and writes it into the memory of the

146
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:38,040
decode machine.

147
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:41,640
Neither processor touches the data and

148
00:05:38,040 --> 00:05:44,000
neither operating system is involved.

149
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:46,400
Storing the cache at eight or four bits

150
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,400
instead of 16 trims it further before it

151
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:50,600
crosses.

152
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:52,600
Decode writes the rest of the answer one

153
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:54,560
token at a time.

154
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:56,960
Every token begins with a read of the

155
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:58,280
active weights, about 30 billion

156
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:01,160
parameters.

157
00:05:58,280 --> 00:06:04,240
At 4 bits each, 30 billion parameters

158
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:06,640
come to 15 GB pulled out of memory.

159
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:08,400
Those parameters do about 60 billion

160
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:12,280
operations of arithmetic on the way

161
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:15,080
past. 60 billion operations over 15 GB

162
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:18,400
is four operations per byte. The chip

163
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:21,040
can sustain 1,250.

164
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:24,080
Each token the model writes adds its own

165
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:26,320
70 KB to the cache. The next read is

166
00:06:24,080 --> 00:06:28,280
therefore slightly larger than the read

167
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:30,400
before it.

168
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:32,560
Continuous batching puts this request

169
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:34,960
through the same forward pass as every

170
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:37,760
other request in flight. One read of the

171
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:39,919
weights then serves all of them.

172
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:42,240
Speculative decoding has a small draft

173
00:06:39,919 --> 00:06:44,280
model proposed several tokens ahead,

174
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:45,600
which the large model checks in a single

175
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:47,880
pass.

176
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:50,120
The forward pass carrying this request

177
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:53,520
therefore holds many sequences, each

178
00:06:50,120 --> 00:06:55,960
with several proposed tokens attached.

179
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:58,600
Every token that decode produces passes

180
00:06:55,960 --> 00:07:00,640
one more step before it is chosen.

181
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:03,280
The answer to this request has to be a

182
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:05,400
tool call, so the engine removes every

183
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:08,440
token that does not fit the tool call

184
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:10,600
format. After an opening brace, only the

185
00:07:08,440 --> 00:07:11,760
field names the format allows are still

186
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:14,800
available.

187
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:17,600
The model picks from what is left.

188
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:19,840
The tokens then reach the user.

189
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:22,200
The wait before the first token is time

190
00:07:19,840 --> 00:07:23,880
to first token, and it covers everything

191
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:26,000
from the gateway through prefill

192
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:28,280
producing that token.

193
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,600
The gap between the tokens after that is

194
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:32,800
time per output token.

195
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:34,840
Everything decode does happens inside

196
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:36,760
that gap.

197
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:39,815
When the last token of the tool call is

198
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:40,000
sent, the request releases its slot,

199
00:07:39,815 --> 00:07:42,240
>> [music]

200
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,320
>> and the decode machine gives that slot

201
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:47,520
to a request that was waiting.

202
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:50,400
This brings us to the end of this LLM

203
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:52,360
inference engineering series.

204
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:54,520
Most of the LLM inference stack this

205
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:55,680
series described did not exist 5 years

206
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:58,480
ago.

207
00:07:55,680 --> 00:08:01,440
Paged caches, latent attention, the

208
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:05,160
prefill and decode split, and 4-bit

209
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:07,320
numerics were all published after 2022.

210
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:10,640
The memory wall they were built against

211
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:13,640
was named in 1995, more than 20 years

212
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:16,400
before the transformer existed.

213
00:08:13,640 --> 00:08:18,280
Inference has grown to about 2/3 of all

214
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:20,840
AI compute.

215
00:08:18,280 --> 00:08:23,080
At the same time, many totally different

216
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:25,240
approaches are being tried.

217
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:27,680
Every technique that fights decode

218
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:29,120
assumes the model writes one token at a

219
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:32,000
time.

220
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:34,520
Diffusion language models do not.

221
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:37,000
A diffusion model starts from a masked

222
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:39,240
sequence and fills it in over several

223
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,479
passes, predicting many positions at

224
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:43,360
once.

225
00:08:40,479 --> 00:08:45,880
Mercury, a commercial diffusion model,

226
00:08:43,360 --> 00:08:49,960
was independently measured at 1,100

227
00:08:45,880 --> 00:08:51,960
tokens a second on H100s.

228
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:54,640
A model that fills in many positions at

229
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:56,440
once is a different problem. And the

230
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:59,160
stack that serves it will look

231
00:08:56,440 --> 00:09:02,000
different, too.

232
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:02,000
Thanks for watching.

